And before anyone asks - network setting are special in certain ways. Particullary, it's often required to change them gradually, interleaving the system with network equipment changes, just to keep connection to the (remote) system alive. Consider moving management VLAN between interfaces while adding LACP. During such transition restarting the `networkd` is not an option.

Of course noone has to use `networkctl` for such tasks, but what would be the point of implementing all the advanced features then?

@poettering - as for the overlayfs, I would expect the `systemd.volatile=overlay` cmdline wouldn't harm `systemd` logic. However I'm not sure, again, if that's even overlay related, just too many things happen here and at least a few seem to need some polishing before going deeper. I'm trying to use `networkd` for a few weeks and it bites... there's a monster hiding after many corners and paragraphs of documentation... Since I'm running out of time till my deadline I'll probably end up with another bunch of `sh` plumbing of deficiencies (#14915!). And this is getting hard and tricky due to the `systemd` constraints (#2741). Honestly I'm not even sure if this is convergent into `systemd`+`networkd` or if it eventually would throw such network setups out of the scope to the "good old times of s-**hell**".
